{
  "id": "80765",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Transition Metals",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Transition metals are elements that have (or that readily form) partially filled "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals. The "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "d"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "-block elements"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Elements in groups 3 through 11 with valence electrons in d orbitals."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3476619869",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2030252098",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " in groups 3 through 11 are transition elements. The "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "f"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "-block elements"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Elements with atomic numbers 58–71 or 90–103 that have valence electrons in f orbitals; they are frequently shown offset below the periodic table; also called inner transition elements)."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "639073791",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1057279580",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", also called "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "inner transition metals"
              },
              {
                "text": " (the lanthanides and actinides), also meet this criterion because the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbital is partially occupied before the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "f"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals. The "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals fill with the copper family (group 11); for this reason, the next family (group 12) are technically not transition elements. However, the group 12 elements do display some of the same chemical properties and are commonly included in discussions of transition metals. Some chemists use the terms "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "transition metals"
              },
              {
                "text": " and "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d-block elements"
              },
              {
                "text": " interchangeably. The "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "f"
              },
              {
                "text": "-block elements are the elements Ce through Lu, which constitute the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "lanthanide series"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Lanthanum and the elements in the first row or the f-block, atomic numbers 57–71; also called lanthanoid series."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2842604227",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "461369348",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", and the elements Th through Lr, which constitute the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "actinide series"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Actinium and the elements in the second row or the f-block, atomic numbers 89–103; also called actinoid series."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1842466778",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3298786358",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". Because lanthanum behaves very much like the lanthanide elements, it is considered a lanthanide element even though its electron configuration makes it the first member of the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "third transition series"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Transition elements in the sixth period of the periodic table (third row of the d-block), atomic numbers 57 and 72–80."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2702000164",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1769916320",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". Similarly, the behavior of actinium means it is part of the actinide series, although its electron configuration makes it the first member of the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "fourth transition series"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Transition elements in the seventh period of the periodic table (fourth row of the d-block), atomic numbers 89 and 104–111."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1052776313",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1633347751",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". Transition metal electron configurations have numerous exceptions to the rules presented in a first-year chemistry text, however the general rules and trends for electron configurations in the ground state explain many of the observed properties for transition metals and their compounds."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3391170637",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
        "id": "362206342",
        "type": "content"
