      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "bup9pyl97n1o2p2",
                "text": "Exceptions to Expected Electron Configurations"
              }
            ],
            "id": "96924304",
            "type": "h2"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "These guidelines allow us to predict the ground-state electron configuration for many elements in the periodic table. However, due to the increasingly small energy differences between valence electron subshells and the more complex interactions between the electrons and the nucleus for larger atoms, many transition metals show exceptions to these rules. For example, the predicted electron configuration for chromium (Cr) is [Ar]4"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "s"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": ", while its actual electron configuration is [Ar]4"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "s"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "1"
              },
              {
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "5"
              },
              {
                "text": ". Similarly, for copper (Cu) the predicted ([Ar]4"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "s"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "9"
              },
              {
                "text": ") and observed ([Ar]4"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "s"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "1"
              },
              {
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "10"
              },
              {
                "text": ") electron configurations also differ. In these cases, the repulsive force between two electrons in the 4"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "s"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbital is greater than between two electrons paired in a 3"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbital so one electron is “shifted” from the 4"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "s"
              },
              {
                "text": " to the 3"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1150596774",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The observed electron configurations of several other elements may be rationalized in terms of lowered energy for half-filled ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "ns"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "1"
              },
              {
                "text": ", "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "np"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ", "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "nd"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "5"
              },
              {
                "text": ", "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "nf"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "7"
              },
              {
                "text": ") or filled ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "ns"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ", "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "np"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "6"
              },
              {
                "text": ", "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "nd"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "10"
              },
              {
                "text": ", "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "nf"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "14"
              },
              {
                "text": ") subshells. Especially in heavier elements, additional effects (that are beyond the scope of this course) lead to anomalous configurations that are slightly different from what is predicted. For example, the second transition series elements have mostly anomalous electron configurations (5"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "s"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "1"
              },
              {
                "text": " not 5"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "s"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "). In general, these anomalies do not have significant chemical consequences, and will not be of concern in this course. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "3020716745",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Use the following method to enter electron configurations in the learning activities below."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3759971290",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Enter the noble gas core with brackets. For example, helium should be entered as [He]."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "332707111",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1279893635",
                "type": "li"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "For superscripts, use the ^ symbol before the number. For example, 2"
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "s"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "2 should be entered as 2s^2."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "am22vgjocq50nbb",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3198029697",
                "type": "li"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Add a space between the noble gas core and between all sublevels. For example, [Ne]3"
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "s"
                      },
                      {
                        "sup": true,
                        "text": "2"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "3"
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "p"
                      },
                      {
                        "sup": true,
                        "text": "4"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " should be entered as [Ne] 3s^2 3p^4."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "j9ngejbbm60dfns",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "876129196",
                "type": "li"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Be sure to list the subshells in order of increasing energy."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "bshbouplbr8j3yl",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1565195078",
                "type": "li"
              }
            ],
            "id": "1713161395",
            "type": "ul"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "1135055272",
            "type": "p"
          }
